From: Veron <spikes/stage3/patches>
Subject: [PATCH] tccgen: a long double constant is not the host's bytes

init_putv chooses how to emit a long double constant by comparing SIZES:

    if (sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
        memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE);

On an x86_64 host targeting arm64 that is TRUE -- both are 16 -- and it copies
the host's x87 bytes verbatim into a slot the target reads as IEEE binary128.
The formats disagree completely:

    x87 80-bit   64-bit mantissa with an EXPLICIT leading 1, 16-bit exponent,
                 padded to 16 bytes
    binary128    15-bit exponent at the top, 112-bit mantissa, IMPLICIT
                 leading 1

MEASURED. `long double f(void){ return 5.008L; }` compiled by an x86_64-hosted
arm64 tcc, reading .data.ro out of the object:

    before   f0a7c64b 378941a0 01400000 00000000
    after    00000000 0000e04f 8d976e12 83400140

and the consequence, run under qemu-aarch64:

    (double)5.008L    before 0x0000000000000000    after 0x4014083126e978d5
    (double)1.5L      before 0x0000000000000000    after 0x3ff8000000000000

Zero, not a mangled value: f3_unpack reads the exponent from the high word,
finds 0 because x87 put it in the low one, takes the underflow branch and
returns zero. Every long double constant in a cross-compiled program is
silently wrong, and nothing notices until something reads one back.

The existing code anticipates this -- "may not work for cross compilers" --
and handles the cases where the sizes DIFFER. What was missing is the case
where the sizes MATCH and the formats do not.

SIZE CANNOT IDENTIFY A FORMAT. LDBL_MANT_DIG can: 64 for x87, 113 for
binary128, 53 for double. When the host is x87 and the target wants
binary128, convert instead of copying -- frexpl the value apart and rebuild
the target encoding. x87 has 64 mantissa bits and binary128 has 113, so this
direction is exact and loses nothing.

NOT ATTEMPTED: a binary128 host emitting x87 constants. Nothing here
cross-compiles that way, and a wrong guess would be silent in exactly the
same manner. It still reaches the existing tcc_error.

TESTED: x86_64 host, arm64 target, output run under qemu-aarch64-static.
NOT TESTED: a native arm64 tcc, where sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE is
true AND the formats agree -- there the guard is false and the original
memcpy path is taken unchanged.

--- a/tccgen.c
+++ b/tccgen.c
@@ -20,6 +20,8 @@
 
 #define USING_GLOBALS
 #include "tcc.h"
+#include <math.h>
+#include <float.h>
 
 /********************************************************/
 /* global variables */
@@ -7894,8 +7896,43 @@
 #endif
                 /* For other platforms it should work natively, but may not work
                    for cross compilers */
+#if defined LDBL_MANT_DIG && LDBL_MANT_DIG == 64
+                if (LDOUBLE_SIZE == 16) {
+                    long double v = vtop->c.ld;
+                    int e = 0, neg = 0;
+                    unsigned long long hi = 0, lo = 0;
+                    if (v != v) {
+                        hi = 0x7fff800000000000ULL;
+                    } else if (v != 0 && v * 0.5L == v) {
+                        hi = 0x7fff000000000000ULL;
+                        if (v < 0) neg = 1;
+                    } else if (v != 0) {
+                        unsigned long long m;
+                        if (v < 0) { neg = 1; v = -v; }
+                        v = frexpl(v, &e);
+                        v = v * 18446744073709551616.0L;
+                        m = (unsigned long long)v;
+                        e = e - 1 + 16383;
+                        /* m holds 64 mantissa bits with x87's EXPLICIT leading
+                           1 at bit 63. binary128's field is 112 bits with an
+                           IMPLICIT leading 1, so drop the top bit and place the
+                           remaining 63 at the top of 112: the high word takes
+                           the first 48, the low word the remaining 15 shifted
+                           to ITS top. */
+                        unsigned long long f = m << 1;   /* drop implicit bit */
+                        hi = ((unsigned long long)e << 48) | (f >> 16);
+                        lo = f << 48;
+                    }
+                    if (neg) hi |= 0x8000000000000000ULL;
+                    write64le(ptr, lo);
+                    write64le(ptr + 8, hi);
+                }
+                else if (sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
+                    memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE);
+#else
                 if (sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
                     memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE);
+#endif
                 else if (sizeof(double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
                     *(double*)ptr = (double)vtop->c.ld;
                 else if (0 == memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE))
